 ## Background
-Originating from the need to accelerate the convergence of 3D Gaussian Splatting and improve geometric fidelity, these methods draw from SLAM systems, variational autoencoders, and pre-trained generative models. Volume Prior Initialization builds on RGB-D SLAM cost functions [S1, unofficial]. SF-VAE adapts spherical folding techniques from neural implicit representations [S1, unofficial]. Generalized Initialization leverages modular frameworks like GauStudio to transfer properties from coarse to fine representations [S2, unofficial]. Random Color Initialization was introduced in text-to-3D pipelines to prevent artifacts from low-fidelity generative priors [S3]. These methods are used in high-fidelity novel-view synthesis, SLAM, and text-to-3D generation.
+The concept of learning-based initialization in Gaussian Splatting emerged as a response to the limitations of random or SfM-based initialization, which often leads to slow convergence and poor structural alignment. Volume Prior Initialization and SF-VAE leverage neural networks to encode geometric priors, drawing from variational autoencoder (VAE) architectures [S1, S1, unofficial]. Generalized Gaussian Splatting Initialization addresses the issue of shape deviation by using pre-computed properties from broader Gaussian splatting models [S2, unofficial]. Random Color Initialization was introduced in the context of text-to-3D generation (e.g., GSGEN) to prevent artifacts from generative models like Point-E [S3]. Convergence Basin Analysis Training methods are rooted in SLAM literature, adapting RGB-D SLAM cost functions to Gaussian map optimization [S1, unofficial].
 
